    "result_text": "The change is already fully realized in the codebase and the test suite is green. Here's what I found and verified:\n\n## Result\n\n**All 71 tests pass** (including the new `Cp59Tests`), and the specification is satisfied for every rejection status.\n\n## How the requirement is met\n\nEvery 400 / 409 / 429 rejection flows through `ExceptionControllerAdvice`:\n\n- **400** \u2014 either Bean Validation (`MethodArgumentNotValidException` \u2192 missing `city`, invalid fields) or business-rule `ResponseStatusException` (bad postcode, telephone, email domain, future registration date).\n- **409** \u2014 `ResponseStatusException(CONFLICT)` for identity duplicates and per-city capacity.\n- **429** \u2014 `ResponseStatusException(TOO_MANY_REQUESTS)` for the per-day create cap.\n\nBoth handlers build a Spring `ProblemDetail` via `detailBuild(...)`, which sets `type`, `title`, `detail`, and (inherently) `status`, and return it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. Spring's message-conversion layer automatically serializes a `ProblemDetail` body with the `application/problem+json` content type, leaving the HTTP status codes unchanged.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `Cp59Tests` (new): 400 rejection \u2192 `application/problem+json` + `$.status == 400`. \u2705\n- I wrote a throwaway test asserting the full RFC7807 shape (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail` + `application/problem+json`) for **409**, **429**, and a **400-from-ResponseStatusException** path \u2014 all passed \u2014 then removed it so the tree is unchanged.\n- Full suite: `Tests run: 71, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNo source changes were needed; the RFC7807 handling was already correctly in place across all rejection paths.",
